maya: Heads-up — Android client hitting a retry loop when our Retrofit client gets a 401. Seen in crash logs from a few users after token refresh. sample stack/logs below, any ideas?

sahil: can you paste the OkHttp config + Authenticator/Interceptor? usually it's either the authenticator reusing the same stale token, or sync vs async refresh race.

maya: sure — minimal parts. note: we call token refresh via suspend function but Authenticator runs on OkHttp thread.
```kotlin
class TokenInterceptor : Interceptor {
  override fun intercept(chain: Interceptor.Chain): Response {
    val token = tokenStore.getAccessToken()
    val req = chain.request().newBuilder()
      .header("Authorization", "Bearer $token")
      .build()
    return chain.proceed(req)
  }
}

class TokenAuthenticator : Authenticator {
  override fun authenticate(route: Route?, response: Response): Request? {
    // naive refresh: blocking network call to auth service
    val newToken = tokenRefresher.refreshSync()
    tokenStore.save(newToken)
    return response.request.newBuilder()
      .header("Authorization", "Bearer $newToken")
      .build()
  }
}

okHttpClient = OkHttpClient.Builder()
  .addInterceptor(TokenInterceptor())
  .authenticator(TokenAuthenticator())
  .retryOnConnectionFailure(true)
  .build()
```

maya: sample log:
```
W/OkHttp: response for call was 401: https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate
I/TokenAuthenticator: starting refresh
I/TokenRefresher: got refresh token, calling endpoint
E/Network: socket timeout after 10s
I/TokenRefresher: retrying refresh
I/TokenAuthenticator: finished refresh, returning updated request
W/OkHttp: response for call was 401: https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate
I/TokenAuthenticator: starting refresh
...
```

eli: looks like refresh is failing intermittently and Authenticator is re-trying for each challenge — OkHttp will re-run authenticate for each challenge so if refresh itself fails transiently you'll get a loop. Also if the refreshed token was invalid or refresh returned 200 but token still rejected, infinite retry can happen.

ryan: two quick patterns we've used: (1) make Authenticator only attempt refresh once per original request using a marker header; (2) perform token refresh outside of Authenticator (synchronized path) and let Interceptor add header with latest token.

sahil: example for (1): in authenticate, check request.header("X-Auth-Attempt") and if present, return null to stop further retries.
```kotlin
override fun authenticate(route: Route?, response: Response): Request? {
  val prior = response.request.header("X-Auth-Attempt")
  if (prior != null) return null // avoid loop
  val newToken = try { tokenRefresher.refreshSync() } catch (e: Exception) { return null }
  return response.request.newBuilder()
    .header("Authorization", "Bearer $newToken")
    .header("X-Auth-Attempt", "1")
    .build()
}
```

eli: also consider limiting refresh attempts via an atomic counter keyed by request tag or user id.

maya: good call. also curious about timeouts — we set call timeout 30s, but token refresh stuck on socket timeout 10s. Could the refresh retry cause interleaved auth attempts?

ryan: yes, if refresh function does its own retries or sleeps, okHttp threads can hang and multiple parallel requests may all try to refresh simultaneously (thundering herd). We usually do a single-flight refresh: first caller refreshes while others wait for result.

sahil: sample single-flight (Kotlin):
```kotlin
private val mutex = Mutex()
private var refreshing: Deferred<String?>? = null

suspend fun getFreshToken(): String? {
  mutex.withLock {
    if (refreshing == null) {
      refreshing = CoroutineScope(Dispatchers.IO).async { refreshSync() }
    }
  }
  return try { refreshing?.await() } finally { mutex.withLock { refreshing = null } }
}
```

maya: are you saying use Coroutine single-flight and call that from interceptor (suspend) — but Interceptor is blocking. any tips?

eli: for Retrofit you can use an Interceptor that calls a blocking version of single-flight (e.g., runBlocking with short window) or pre-warm token before burst of calls. Alternatively, keep Authenticator thin (only handles the rare case) and perform refresh proactively when receiving 401 in app logic.

ryan: small practical fix that worked for us: 1) add X-Auth-Attempt marker to auth requests; 2) set tokenRefresher to do at most 2 retries with exponential backoff and fail fast; 3) ensure authenticate returns null if refresh fails — so the original 401 surfaces instead of infinite loop.

maya: implemented marker header + early return. also reduced retryOnConnectionFailure to false for calls that mustn't retry at network layer. pushing hotfix now.

